  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>C8.S2 — Two reflections, one rotation</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<div style="background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-left:5px solid #026573;border-radius:8px;padding:16px 22px;margin-bottom:14px;"><p style="color:#026573;font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:bold;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;margin:0;">C8.S2 — The geometry of the Grover iterate</p></div><p>(a) The product of reflections about two lines meeting at angle \(\alpha\) is a rotation. Give the rotation angle in terms of <code>alpha</code>.</p><p>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</p><p>(b) Give the two eigenvalues of the Grover iterate restricted to the good/bad plane, as an ordered list, in terms of <code>theta</code> (use <code>%e</code> and <code>%i</code>).</p><p>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</p><p>(c) Amplitude estimation with \(K\) applications of \(G\) estimates \(a=\sin^2\theta\) to additive error scaling as \(K^{-\gamma}\) at leading order. Give \(\gamma\).</p><p>\(\gamma=\) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) \(2\alpha\). Applied with \(\alpha=\theta\) (the angle between \(|\text{bad}\rangle\) and \(|\psi\rangle\)) this gives the \(2\theta\) rotation per Grover step.</p><p>(b) A planar rotation by \(2\theta\) has eigenvalues \(e^{\pm 2i\theta}\).</p><p>(c) \(\gamma=1\): the error is \(O(1/K)\), quadratically better than the \(O(1/\sqrt K)\) of classical Monte Carlo. This is the same Heisenberg scaling as phase estimation in Chapter 6 — unsurprising, since amplitude estimation <em>is</em> phase estimation applied to \(G\).</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
