  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>C8.S3 — Optimality and what a quadratic speed-up buys</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<div style="background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-left:5px solid #026573;border-radius:8px;padding:16px 22px;margin-bottom:14px;"><p style="color:#026573;font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:bold;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;margin:0;">C8.S3 — Lower bounds and cryptographic consequences</p></div><p>(a) Give the BBBV lower bound on the number of oracle queries needed to search \(N\) items with bounded error, as a function of <code>N</code> (give the growth rate, e.g. <code>sqrt(N)</code>).</p><p>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</p><p>(b) A symmetric cipher has a \(b\)-bit key. Give the number of Grover iterations needed for exhaustive key search, in terms of <code>b</code> (leading order, ignoring the \(\pi/4\)).</p><p>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</p><p>(c) Give the key length \(b\) needed so that a Grover attack costs at least \(2^{ {@s@} }\) iterations.</p><p>\(b=\) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) \(\Omega(\sqrt N)\), by the hybrid argument: after \(T\) queries the algorithm can only have distinguished the flipped oracle if \(T=\Omega(\sqrt N)\). Grover matches it, so unstructured search is settled up to constants.</p><p>(b) \(N=2^{b}\), so \(\sqrt N=2^{b/2}\) iterations.</p><p>(c) \(2^{b/2}\ge 2^{ {@s@} }\) requires \(b\ge {@ta3@}\).</p><p>This is the origin of the "double your symmetric key length" rule for post-quantum security: AES-256 retains roughly 128 bits of security against Grover. Note the rule is conservative — Grover parallelises badly, and the fault-tolerant constant factors are enormous, so the effective margin is larger than the bare exponent suggests.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
